When you call Stream.collect(Collectors.toList()) the stream gathers every element into a fresh List object. The collector does not promise a specific List implementation, nor does it guarantee that the list can be changed or used safely by many threads at once. In practice the result is usually an ArrayList, but you should treat it as an opaque list unless you need more control. If you need a mutable ArrayList you can use collect(toCollection(ArrayList::new)). The list keeps the encounter order of the stream when the source is ordered. Unmodifiable views can be created later with Collections.unmodifiableList, but the objects inside the list may still be mutable. This behavior is defined in Java 8 and later, and the same rules apply in Java 17.

```java
List<String> names = Stream.of("Alice", "Bob", "Carol")
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// names is a list with the three strings in order.
// To guarantee a mutable ArrayList:
List<String> mutable = Stream.of("Alice", "Bob")
    .collect(Collectors.toCollection(ArrayList::new));
```

Collecting database query results into a list for further processing, while remembering that the list may be unmodifiable and its elements can still change if they are mutable objects.
